# PowerShell scripts keep CRLF in the working tree.
#
# Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads a .ps1 with LF endings, so this is not about
# execution -- it is about the round trip. git normalising CRLF to LF on commit
# and back on checkout makes every .ps1 show as modified the moment it is
# touched, which is noise in `git status` and noise in commit-session.ps1, whose
# whole job is to tell real changes from incidental ones.
#
# Prompted by git warning "CRLF will be replaced by LF the next time Git touches
# it" during a commit on 2026-09-14.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# .env files are read by docker compose inside a Linux container. CRLF there
# ends up INSIDE the variable value -- CB_PASSWORD would carry a trailing
# carriage return and authentication would fail with a correct password, which
# is among the least obvious failures available.
*.env    text eol=lf
.env.*   text eol=lf
